@page "/events/tutorials/01-setup"

<TutorialDetail Tutorials="TutorialSet.Events" Slug="01-setup">
<p><strong>Goal:</strong> add RForge.Blazor.Events to a Blazor Web App and build a first component that uses it.</p>

<p>This tutorial uses a Blazor Web App with <strong>Auto</strong> interactivity, which has a server project (<code>MyApp</code>) and a client project (<code>MyApp.Client</code>). That covers every render mode. If your app only uses Interactive Server, you have a single project: put the "Client" files in it and skip the client-specific steps.</p>

<h2 id="1-lay-out-the-project-recommended-not-required">1. Lay out the project (recommended, not required)</h2>

<p>This is the layout the tutorials use. It is a recommendation: you can organize files however suits your app. Only these four things are required:</p>

<ol>
<li>Every project that contains event-aware components references <code>RForge.Blazor.Events</code> (NuGet doesn't pass analyzers through project references, so <a href="#2-reference-the-package">step 2</a> adds it to both projects).</li>
<li>Every host that runs interactive components calls <code>AddRForgeEvents</code>, with the same configuration (<a href="#4-register-the-hub-in-both-hosts">step 4</a>).</li>
<li>State and event types live in a project that your WebAssembly code can see.</li>
<li>Event-aware components start with <code>@@inherits RfEventComponent</code>, and keep <code>[Subscribe]</code> and <code>[UseState]</code> members in the code-behind (<code>.razor.cs</code>), not in an <code>@@code</code> block.</li>
</ol>

<p>The recommended layout:</p>

<pre><code>MyApp/                                  server project (Blazor Web App host)
├── Program.cs                          registers the hub for the server
├── Services/                           server-only services (Scoped) that publish or subscribe
└── Components/                         layout, App.razor, server-only pages
MyApp.Client/                           everything that might run in WebAssembly
├── Program.cs                          registers the hub for the WebAssembly runtime
├── EventHubSetup.cs                    ONE hub configuration shared by both Program.cs files
├── AppJsonContext.cs                   JSON metadata for persisted state (tutorial 08)
├── GlobalUsings.cs                     global using RForge.Blazor.Events;
├── _Imports.razor                      @@using RForge.Blazor.Events
├── Features/
│   └── &lt;Feature&gt;/
│       ├── &lt;Feature&gt;State.cs           IState records
│       ├── &lt;Feature&gt;Events.cs          IEvent records
│       └── Components/
│           ├── Something.razor         markup, starts with @@inherits RfEventComponent
│           └── Something.razor.cs      code-behind with [Subscribe] / [UseState]
└── Pages/                              routable pages that run in WebAssembly / Auto</code></pre>

<p>Why we suggest these locations:</p>

<div class="table-container"><table class="table is-striped">
<thead>
<tr><th>File</th><th>Location</th><th>Why</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>State and event types</td><td><code>MyApp.Client/Features/…</code></td><td>WebAssembly and Auto components need them, and the server project references the client, so both sides can use them. Types placed in the server project are invisible to WebAssembly.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Components that use the hub</td><td><code>MyApp.Client/Features/&lt;Feature&gt;/Components/</code></td><td>Same reason. The server can still render them with <code>InteractiveServer</code>. Keeping them next to their state and events makes a feature easy to find, move or delete.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>EventHubSetup.cs</code></td><td><code>MyApp.Client/</code></td><td>The server and the WebAssembly runtime each have their <strong>own</strong> hub. One shared configuration method keeps them identical.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Server-only services</td><td><code>MyApp/Services/</code></td><td>Anything using databases, secrets or server APIs must not ship to the browser.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>

<h2 id="2-reference-the-package">2. Reference the package</h2>

<p>Add the <a href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/RForge.Blazor.Events"><code>RForge.Blazor.Events</code></a> package to <strong>both</strong> projects:</p>

<pre><code>dotnet add MyApp.Client package RForge.Blazor.Events
dotnet add MyApp package RForge.Blazor.Events</code></pre>

<p>or in each <code>.csproj</code>:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;!-- MyApp.Client/MyApp.Client.csproj and MyApp/MyApp.csproj --&gt;
&lt;ItemGroup&gt;
  &lt;PackageReference Include="RForge.Blazor.Events" Version="1.0.0" /&gt;
&lt;/ItemGroup&gt;</code></pre>

<p>The package contains the runtime library <strong>and</strong> the source generator/analyzers, so nothing else is needed.</p>

<p><strong>Why both projects?</strong> The runtime library would reach the server through its reference to <code>MyApp.Client</code>, but NuGet doesn't pass a package's <strong>analyzers</strong> (which include the source generator) through project references. Any project that contains components using <code>[Subscribe]</code> or <code>[UseState]</code> needs its own reference. Referencing the package in both projects also keeps them on the same version.</p>

<blockquote>
<p><strong>Tip:</strong> with several projects, use <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/nuget/consume-packages/central-package-management">Central Package Management</a> (<code>Directory.Packages.props</code>) so the version is defined once.</p>
</blockquote>

<h2 id="3-create-one-shared-configuration">3. Create one shared configuration</h2>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/EventHubSetup.cs
namespace MyApp.Client;

/// &lt;summary&gt;Hub options shared by the server and the WebAssembly host, so both hubs behave the same.&lt;/summary&gt;
public static class EventHubSetup
{
    public static void Configure(RfEventOptions options)
    {
        // Later tutorials add options here, for example:
        //   options.CascadeRootState&lt;ThemeState&gt;();               (tutorial 03)
        //   options.PersistState(AppJsonContext.Default.X);       (tutorial 08)
    }
}</code></pre>

<h2 id="4-register-the-hub-in-both-hosts">4. Register the hub in both hosts</h2>

<pre><code>// MyApp/Program.cs
using MyApp.Client;
using MyApp.Components;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddRazorComponents()
    .AddInteractiveServerComponents()
    .AddInteractiveWebAssemblyComponents();

// One root hub per circuit (Server) and per request (static rendering / prerendering).
builder.Services.AddRForgeEvents(EventHubSetup.Configure);

var app = builder.Build();

if (!app.Environment.IsDevelopment())
{
    app.UseExceptionHandler("/Error", createScopeForErrors: true);
    app.UseHsts();
}
else
{
    app.UseWebAssemblyDebugging();
}

app.UseHttpsRedirection();
app.UseAntiforgery();
app.MapStaticAssets();
app.MapRazorComponents&lt;App&gt;()
    .AddInteractiveServerRenderMode()
    .AddInteractiveWebAssemblyRenderMode()
    .AddAdditionalAssemblies(typeof(MyApp.Client._Imports).Assembly);

app.Run();</code></pre>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Program.cs
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly.Hosting;
using MyApp.Client;

var builder = WebAssemblyHostBuilder.CreateDefault(args);

// The WebAssembly runtime has its own DI container, so it needs its own hub.
builder.Services.AddRForgeEvents(EventHubSetup.Configure);

await builder.Build().RunAsync();</code></pre>

<blockquote>
<p>Forgetting the client registration is the most common setup mistake. Everything works on Server, then fails with <em>"no registered service of type IRfEventHub"</em> when a page switches to WebAssembly.</p>
</blockquote>

<h2 id="5-import-the-namespace">5. Import the namespace</h2>

<p><code>.razor</code> files and <code>.cs</code> files have separate imports, so add both:</p>

<pre><code>@@* MyApp.Client/_Imports.razor *@@
@@using System.Net.Http
@@using System.Net.Http.Json
@@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Forms
@@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Routing
@@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Web
@@using static Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Web.RenderMode
@@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Web.Virtualization
@@using Microsoft.JSInterop
@@using MyApp.Client
@@using RForge.Blazor.Events</code></pre>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/GlobalUsings.cs
global using RForge.Blazor.Events;</code></pre>

<p>In the server project, add <code>@@using RForge.Blazor.Events</code> to <code>MyApp/Components/_Imports.razor</code> if server components use the hub, and <code>using RForge.Blazor.Events;</code> at the top of server services (or a <code>GlobalUsings.cs</code> there too). The client's global using doesn't apply to the server project.</p>

<h2 id="6-build-a-first-event-aware-component">6. Build a first event-aware component</h2>

<p>Two components that share no parent: one edits a name, the other greets it.</p>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Features/Welcome/WelcomeState.cs
namespace MyApp.Client.Features.Welcome;

public sealed record WelcomeState(string Name) : IState&lt;WelcomeState&gt;
{
    public static WelcomeState Initial =&gt; new("stranger");
}</code></pre>

<pre><code>@@* MyApp.Client/Features/Welcome/Components/NameEditor.razor *@@
@@inherits RfEventComponent

&lt;input class="form-control" placeholder="Your name" @@bind="_name" @@bind:event="oninput" @@bind:after="Save" /&gt;</code></pre>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Features/Welcome/Components/NameEditor.razor.cs
namespace MyApp.Client.Features.Welcome.Components;

public partial class NameEditor
{
    private string _name = "";

    private void Save() =&gt; State&lt;WelcomeState&gt;().Set(new WelcomeState(_name.Length == 0 ? "stranger" : _name));
}</code></pre>

<pre><code>@@* MyApp.Client/Features/Welcome/Components/Greeting.razor *@@
@@inherits RfEventComponent

&lt;h2&gt;Hello, @@Welcome.Name!&lt;/h2&gt;</code></pre>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Features/Welcome/Components/Greeting.razor.cs
namespace MyApp.Client.Features.Welcome.Components;

public partial class Greeting
{
    [UseState]
    private partial WelcomeState Welcome { get; }
}</code></pre>

<pre><code>@@* MyApp.Client/Pages/WelcomePage.razor *@@
@@page "/welcome"
@@rendermode InteractiveAuto
@@using MyApp.Client.Features.Welcome.Components

&lt;Greeting /&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
&lt;NameEditor /&gt;</code></pre>

<p>Run the app and open <code>/welcome</code>. Typing in the box updates the greeting, although neither component knows the other exists.</p>

<h2 id="7-check-that-the-generator-ran">7. Check that the generator ran</h2>

<p><code>[UseState] private partial WelcomeState Welcome { get; }</code> has no body. The source generator writes it. To see the generated code:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Visual Studio / Rider:</strong> <em>Dependencies → Analyzers → RForge.Blazor.Events.Generators → …Greeting.Hub.g.cs</em></li>
<li><strong>Any editor:</strong> add <code>&lt;EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles&gt;true&lt;/EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles&gt;</code> to the csproj and look under <code>obj/…/generated/</code>.</li>
</ul>

<p>If the generator isn't running, the build fails with <strong>CS9248</strong> ("partial property must have an implementation part"). That almost always means the project containing the component has no <code>RForge.Blazor.Events</code> package reference of its own (see <a href="#2-reference-the-package">step 2</a>).</p>

<h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>

<ul>
<li><code>AddRForgeEvents</code> registers a <strong>Scoped</strong> root hub: one per circuit on Server, one per app instance in WebAssembly.</li>
<li><code>RfEventComponent</code> finds the hub, runs the generated <code>RegisterSubscriptions</code> before your <code>OnInitialized</code>, re-renders the component when state it reads changes, and unsubscribes on dispose.</li>
<li>State types are created lazily from their <code>Initial</code> value the first time anything asks for them. No registration is needed.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="-best-practices">✅ Best practices</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Put state, events and event-aware components in the Client project.</strong> Then they work in every render mode.</li>
<li><strong>Use one <code>EventHubSetup.Configure</code> for both hosts.</strong> Two hand-written configurations drift apart.</li>
<li><strong>One feature folder per concept</strong> (<code>Features/Cart</code>, <code>Features/Welcome</code>). State, events and components that change together live together.</li>
<li><strong>Always pair <code>.razor</code> with <code>.razor.cs</code></strong> for event-aware components, even small ones. The attributes only work in the code-behind, and consistency means nobody has to remember which components are special.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="common-mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>

<div class="table-container"><table class="table is-striped">
<thead>
<tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>CS0263: Partial declarations … must not specify different base classes</code></td><td>Base class declared only in the code-behind</td><td>Add <code>@@inherits RfEventComponent</code> to the <code>.razor</code> file</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>RFEVT001</code></td><td><code>[Subscribe]</code>/<code>[UseState]</code> inside an <code>@@code</code> block</td><td>Move the member to the <code>.razor.cs</code> file</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>RFEVT003</code></td><td>Attributes used in a component that doesn't derive from <code>RfEventComponent</code></td><td>Add <code>@@inherits RfEventComponent</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>CS9248</code> on a <code>[UseState]</code> property</td><td>The project has no <code>RForge.Blazor.Events</code> package reference of its own, so the generator didn't run</td><td>Add the package to that project (<a href="#2-reference-the-package">step 2</a>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Works on Server, fails on WebAssembly</td><td>Hub not registered in <code>MyApp.Client/Program.cs</code></td><td>See <a href="#4-register-the-hub-in-both-hosts">step 4</a></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>

